    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "To assess the effects of fault geometry on displacement fields, numerical models are used to compute displacement fields for idealized faults and a set of faults interpreted from 3D seismic data. The displacement fields are used to construct synthetic structure contour maps that can be compared to mapped horizons. Modeling efforts from this study indicate that displacement fields associated with closely spaced opposed dipping normal faults are more complex than those associated with isolated faults or echelon arrays of synthetic faults. The results and methodologies presented here represent ongoing research, for which the ultimate goal is to develop tools that can be applied to reduce uncertainty associated with interpreting fault geometries in 3D seismic data.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
